Subject: [PATCH] software: fix scissor Y double-flip causing scissored
 content to render nowhere at all

swScissor() (src/external/rlsw.h, the software rasterizer backing
GRAPHICS_API_OPENGL_SOFTWARE) computed its own clip-space Y range
using "1.0f - 2*y/height" - a top-down-to-NDC conversion. But the "y"
it receives has already been converted from top-down screen space to
bottom-up by BeginScissorMode() (src/rcore.c, shared across every
raylib platform: "rlScissor(x, CORE.Window.currentFbo.height - (y +
height), width, height)"), the same convention real hardware OpenGL's
own glScissor() expects.

Applying a second, independent flip on top of that already-converted
value lands the scissor's own clip-space Y range in the opposite half
of NDC space from where the actual geometry being scissored gets
projected (that geometry's own screen-to-NDC conversion has no
equivalent second flip). Confirmed directly: for a 900x700 window
with BeginScissorMode(280, 30, 240, 140), swScissor() received
y=530 (700 - (30+140), i.e. already bottom-up) - the old formula
produced scClipMin/Max Y of [-0.914, -0.514] (bottom half of NDC),
while a rectangle drawn well inside that same nominal scissor region
(at screen Y 50-150) projects to NDC Y [0.571, 0.857] (top half) -
zero overlap. Every vertex of scissored content then fails the
IS_INSIDE_SCISSOR_Y_MIN/MAX test and gets discarded entirely - not
clipped at the true edges, simply never drawn at all, regardless of
whether it's anywhere near the scissor's actual boundary.

Fixed by using the same bottom-up-to-NDC formula ("2*y/height - 1.0f")
already used for the X axis and for the general viewport/projection
math elsewhere in this file, instead of re-flipping an axis that
BeginScissorMode() already flipped once.

Upstream-Status: Backport [https://github.com/raysan5/raylib/commit/7a247ff40fc5e77243b6e0af856437d2e6231c31]

Found and fixed independently here before discovering this - an
upstream contributor (Thiago Mucci) had already found and fixed the
exact same bug, merged as raylib PR #5976 on 2026-07-13, with the
identical formula change. That fix landed after this layer's own
pinned SRCREV (dbc56a87, which is raylib's own 6.0 release tag itself)
and isn't included in any tagged release yet as of this writing -
kept as a local backport rather than bumping SRCREV past the 6.0 tag
to an untagged development commit (a materially bigger, less
predictable jump than this one specific, already-verified line).
Drop this patch once this project's own raylib recipe moves to a
release that includes 7a247ff4 or later (check with
"git merge-base --is-ancestor 7a247ff4 <new-tag>" against raylib's own
upstream repo).

diff --git a/src/external/rlsw.h b/src/external/rlsw.h
index 8f7ab440..ac4f4351 100644
--- a/src/external/rlsw.h
+++ b/src/external/rlsw.h
@@ -4210,8 +4210,22 @@ void swScissor(int x, int y, int width, int height)
 
     RLSW.scClipMin[0] = (2.0f*(float)RLSW.scMin[0]/(float)RLSW.vpSize[0]) - 1.0f;
     RLSW.scClipMax[0] = (2.0f*(float)RLSW.scMax[0]/(float)RLSW.vpSize[0]) - 1.0f;
-    RLSW.scClipMax[1] = 1.0f - (2.0f*(float)RLSW.scMin[1]/(float)RLSW.vpSize[1]);
-    RLSW.scClipMin[1] = 1.0f - (2.0f*(float)RLSW.scMax[1]/(float)RLSW.vpSize[1]);
+    // Y flipped without a second inversion: BeginScissorMode() (rcore.c,
+    // shared across every raylib platform) already converts the
+    // incoming top-down screen Y into a bottom-up value before calling
+    // rlScissor()/here - "y" received by this function is already
+    // measured from the bottom, the same convention real hardware
+    // OpenGL's own glScissor() expects. The previous "1.0f - ..." form
+    // applied a SECOND, independent top-down-style flip on top of
+    // that already-converted value, landing the scissor's own clip-
+    // space Y range in the opposite half of NDC space from where the
+    // actual geometry being scissored gets projected (that geometry's
+    // own screen-to-NDC conversion has no equivalent second flip) -
+    // meaning content meant to be visible within the scissor region
+    // instead failed every "is this vertex inside the scissor" check
+    // and got discarded entirely, not just clipped at the true edges.
+    RLSW.scClipMin[1] = (2.0f*(float)RLSW.scMin[1]/(float)RLSW.vpSize[1]) - 1.0f;
+    RLSW.scClipMax[1] = (2.0f*(float)RLSW.scMax[1]/(float)RLSW.vpSize[1]) - 1.0f;
 }
 
 void swClearColor(float r, float g, float b, float a)
